1
00:00:00,470 --> 00:00:02,650
Welcome to the chapter on cardiac pathology.

2
00:00:02,650 --> 00:00:05,170
The first discussion is ischemic heart disease.

3
00:00:05,170 --> 00:00:06,450
This is a group of syndromes.

4
00:00:06,450 --> 00:00:07,730
We're gonna cover four in all

5
00:00:07,730 --> 00:00:09,930
that are related to myocardial ischemia.

6
00:00:09,930 --> 00:00:12,010
Remember that ischemia refers to a decrease

7
00:00:12,010 --> 00:00:13,490
in blood flow to an organ.

8
00:00:13,490 --> 00:00:15,330
It's usually due to atherosclerosis.

9
00:00:15,330 --> 00:00:17,850
If you've got atherosclerosis in the coronary artery,

10
00:00:17,850 --> 00:00:19,570
you decrease the amount of blood flow

11
00:00:19,570 --> 00:00:21,210
going to the myocardium.

12
00:00:21,210 --> 00:00:23,930
Therefore, the risk factors for ischemic heart disease

13
00:00:23,930 --> 00:00:27,170
are gonna be very similar to those of atherosclerosis,

14
00:00:27,170 --> 00:00:29,290
and it so happens that the incidence

15
00:00:29,290 --> 00:00:31,330
of ischemic heart disease increases with age,

16
00:00:31,330 --> 00:00:33,850
just as the incidence of atherosclerosis

17
00:00:33,850 --> 00:00:35,610
also increases with age.

18
00:00:35,610 --> 00:00:38,010
The first of these disorders is stable angina.

19
00:00:38,010 --> 00:00:40,570
Angina refers to chest pain that is reversible.

20
00:00:40,570 --> 00:00:42,730
The term stable here implies that the patient

21
00:00:42,730 --> 00:00:44,490
does not have chest pain at rest,

22
00:00:44,490 --> 00:00:46,810
but instead develops chest pain with exertion

23
00:00:46,810 --> 00:00:48,290
or emotional stress.

24
00:00:48,290 --> 00:00:50,770
The idea here is that if this is the coronary artery,

25
00:00:50,770 --> 00:00:52,690
which is feeding the myocardium,

26
00:00:52,690 --> 00:00:54,690
then the patients have atherosclerosis

27
00:00:54,690 --> 00:00:56,770
of the coronary artery.

28
00:00:56,770 --> 00:00:59,970
This then decreases the blood flow to the myocardium.

29
00:00:59,970 --> 00:01:01,330
The patient would be fine at rest.

30
00:01:01,330 --> 00:01:03,090
However, when there is exertion,

31
00:01:03,090 --> 00:01:05,090
you won't be able to provide enough blood

32
00:01:05,090 --> 00:01:07,650
to the myocardium, and hence there would be injury

33
00:01:07,650 --> 00:01:10,610
to the myocardium resulting in chest pain.

34
00:01:10,610 --> 00:01:13,650
Now, it's very high yield to note that when you get stable angina,

35
00:01:13,650 --> 00:01:16,610
you have reversible injury to the myocytes.

36
00:01:16,610 --> 00:01:18,050
So the cells do not die.

37
00:01:18,050 --> 00:01:20,610
They simply undergo reversible injury.

38
00:01:20,610 --> 00:01:22,690
And remember from chapter one of this text

39
00:01:22,690 --> 00:01:26,370
that the hallmark of reversible injury is cellular swelling.

40
00:01:26,370 --> 00:01:29,730
One more point to note is that when the patients have atherosclerosis,

41
00:01:29,730 --> 00:01:33,570
they're only going to get angina when there's greater than 70% stenosis.

42
00:01:33,570 --> 00:01:35,650
If there's less than 70% stenosis,

43
00:01:35,650 --> 00:01:39,330
the patients are relatively asymptomatic, even with exertion.

44
00:01:39,330 --> 00:01:41,170
When patients present with stable angina,

45
00:01:41,170 --> 00:01:44,610
they'll have chest pain that radiates to the left arm or jaw.

46
00:01:44,610 --> 00:01:47,970
Important to note that this chest pain will last less than 20 minutes.

47
00:01:47,970 --> 00:01:53,250
Now, that's about the amount of time that the myocardium can withstand a lack of blood flow.

48
00:01:53,250 --> 00:01:57,010
After that, you'd get irreversible injury and cell death.

49
00:01:57,010 --> 00:01:59,890
And so the chest pain classically will last less than 20 minutes.

50
00:01:59,890 --> 00:02:03,730
The patients may also have diaphoresis or sweating and shortness of breath.

51
00:02:03,730 --> 00:02:08,290
Were an EKG to be performed, the EKG would show ST segment depression.

52
00:02:08,290 --> 00:02:15,090
Now, the reason being is that here is the epicardium and then the myocardium and the endocardium.

53
00:02:15,090 --> 00:02:19,970
Remember that these are the three layers of the heart, epicardium, myocardium, and endocardium.

54
00:02:19,970 --> 00:02:23,490
The coronary arteries, they begin in the epicardium up here,

55
00:02:23,490 --> 00:02:27,890
and then they eventually dive down in order to feed all three layers of the heart.

56
00:02:27,890 --> 00:02:31,810
Now, if you decrease the amount of blood flow going through the coronary artery,

57
00:02:31,810 --> 00:02:37,970
then this region here, right underneath the endocardium, is the most susceptible to ischemic damage.

58
00:02:37,970 --> 00:02:40,370
And so that's exactly what's going to happen in stable angina.

59
00:02:40,370 --> 00:02:44,130
You're going to get subendocardial right underneath the endocardium.

60
00:02:44,130 --> 00:02:46,610
You're going to get subendocardial ischemia.

61
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:52,050
And the hallmark of subendocardial ischemia or damage on an EKG is ST segment depression.

62
00:02:52,690 --> 00:02:56,130
Now, the chest pain in stable angina will be relieved by rest,

63
00:02:56,130 --> 00:03:00,050
and that's because you decrease the demand of the myocardium by resting,

64
00:03:00,050 --> 00:03:05,330
which then will decrease the amount of blood flow that needs to be provided to the myocardium.

65
00:03:05,330 --> 00:03:08,050
Another way to relieve the chest pain would be nitroglycerin.

66
00:03:08,050 --> 00:03:12,770
Remember that nitroglycerin has the ability to vasodilate both arteries and veins.

67
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:16,370
Its major mechanism of action is the vasodilation of veins,

68
00:03:16,370 --> 00:03:19,570
which would then decrease the amount of blood that's returning to the heart.

69
00:03:19,570 --> 00:03:22,130
And so then you would decrease the preload on the heart,

70
00:03:22,130 --> 00:03:25,490
which would then decrease the stress on the myocardium of the heart.

71
00:03:26,130 --> 00:03:28,290
Unstable angina is chest pain.

72
00:03:28,290 --> 00:03:31,890
That's reversible, but it's unstable, i.e. it occurs at rest.

73
00:03:32,450 --> 00:03:36,530
And this is due to rupture of the atherosclerotic plaque with thrombosis,

74
00:03:36,530 --> 00:03:42,130
but an incomplete occlusion of a coronary artery, high yield, incomplete occlusion.

75
00:03:42,130 --> 00:03:43,010
Here's the idea.

76
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:45,970
You've got a coronary artery going into the myocardium,

77
00:03:46,690 --> 00:03:50,610
and this coronary artery has atherosclerosis.

78
00:03:51,250 --> 00:03:55,250
Imagine if there's rupture, which would usually occur at the neck here or here,

79
00:03:55,250 --> 00:04:00,930
then this would expose the subendothelial collagen and the necrotic material within the plaque,

80
00:04:00,930 --> 00:04:04,370
which would then allow for activation of thrombosis.

81
00:04:04,370 --> 00:04:08,770
Now thrombosis would occur, but it would only partially occlude this vessel.

82
00:04:08,770 --> 00:04:12,050
That would then allow for a small amount of blood to flow through,

83
00:04:12,050 --> 00:04:14,930
which would keep the myocardium alive.

84
00:04:14,930 --> 00:04:17,730
However, the patient would have chest pain at rest.

85
00:04:17,730 --> 00:04:20,770
Now again, important to note, you get incomplete occlusion.

86
00:04:20,770 --> 00:04:22,050
That's super high yield.

87
00:04:22,050 --> 00:04:25,810
And that this again would represent reversible injury to the myocytes.

88
00:04:25,810 --> 00:04:28,450
On EKG, you would see ST-segment depression,

89
00:04:28,450 --> 00:04:31,970
and that's because you would have predominantly subendocardial ischemia.

90
00:04:31,970 --> 00:04:33,570
It's relieved by nitroglycerin.

91
00:04:33,570 --> 00:04:36,530
Again, vasodilation would result in decreased preload,

92
00:04:36,530 --> 00:04:40,130
which would then decrease the amount of work the myocardium had to do.

93
00:04:40,130 --> 00:04:42,690
And there's a high risk of progression to MI.

94
00:04:42,690 --> 00:04:46,130
Now imagine, here's an example where you can see a coronary artery.

95
00:04:46,130 --> 00:04:48,210
Now there's been rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque,

96
00:04:48,210 --> 00:04:50,530
and you can clearly see that there's a thrombus here.

97
00:04:50,530 --> 00:04:53,330
Now this circumstance has a very high risk of progression to MI,

98
00:04:53,330 --> 00:04:57,410
because it would be very easy for this thrombus to completely occlude the vessel.

99
00:04:57,410 --> 00:04:59,010
And when you completely occlude the vessel,

100
00:04:59,010 --> 00:05:01,810
that would then result in myocardial infarction.

101
00:05:01,810 --> 00:05:03,970
The third type of angina, again, reversible chest pain,

102
00:05:03,970 --> 00:05:05,650
is called prinzmental angina.

103
00:05:05,650 --> 00:05:08,930
The idea here is that you get vasospasm of the coronary artery.

104
00:05:08,930 --> 00:05:10,610
So again, here's the coronary artery.

105
00:05:10,610 --> 00:05:12,530
This is going into the myocardium.

106
00:05:12,530 --> 00:05:16,130
And you get vasospasm so that this artery clamps down.

107
00:05:16,130 --> 00:05:17,410
Now when this artery clamps down,

108
00:05:17,410 --> 00:05:19,730
it's going to completely cut the blood supply.

109
00:05:19,730 --> 00:05:24,690
And that cutting of the blood supply will then result in episodes of chest pain

110
00:05:24,690 --> 00:05:26,850
that are unrelated to exertion.

111
00:05:26,850 --> 00:05:28,210
Whenever the vessel clamps down,

112
00:05:28,210 --> 00:05:30,290
that's when the patient's going to have chest pain.

113
00:05:30,290 --> 00:05:34,210
Now again, important to note that this still represents reversible injury to the myocytes.

114
00:05:34,210 --> 00:05:37,010
We have not yet caused death of the myocytes.

115
00:05:37,570 --> 00:05:40,130
And the EKG would show ST segment elevation.

116
00:05:40,130 --> 00:05:43,090
Now the reason being is that if this is the epicardium,

117
00:05:43,090 --> 00:05:44,690
this is the myocardium,

118
00:05:44,690 --> 00:05:46,450
and this is the endocardium,

119
00:05:46,450 --> 00:05:48,530
and what's happening here is that in the epicardium,

120
00:05:48,530 --> 00:05:50,130
you've got the coronary artery,

121
00:05:50,130 --> 00:05:52,290
and this is completely clamping down.

122
00:05:52,290 --> 00:05:54,610
When this completely clamps down for a short period of time,

123
00:05:54,610 --> 00:05:57,010
you will cut blood supply to the entire wall,

124
00:05:57,010 --> 00:05:58,530
i.e. transmural.

125
00:05:58,530 --> 00:06:00,210
And when you have transmural damage,

126
00:06:00,210 --> 00:06:03,810
the classic finding is going to be ST segment elevation.

127
00:06:03,810 --> 00:06:06,130
Now this would also be relieved by nitroglycerin,

128
00:06:06,130 --> 00:06:08,610
which would decrease the preload on the heart.

129
00:06:08,610 --> 00:06:11,250
In addition, calcium channel blockers can be used,

130
00:06:11,250 --> 00:06:14,290
which would help to relieve the coronary artery vasospasm.

131
00:06:15,650 --> 00:06:19,650
The next of the ischemic heart disease syndromes is myocardial infarction.

132
00:06:19,650 --> 00:06:22,450
This is when you get necrosis of the cardiac myocytes,

133
00:06:22,450 --> 00:06:24,690
so you get irreversible injury and cell death.

134
00:06:25,250 --> 00:06:29,010
The classic way by which this occurs is rupture of an atherosclerotic plaque

135
00:06:29,010 --> 00:06:31,090
with complete high yield,

136
00:06:31,090 --> 00:06:34,450
with complete occlusion of the coronary artery.

137
00:06:34,450 --> 00:06:37,890
So the idea here is that this is the coronary artery going into the heart.

138
00:06:37,890 --> 00:06:39,170
You've got atherosclerosis.

139
00:06:39,170 --> 00:06:40,610
You get rupture at the neck.

140
00:06:40,610 --> 00:06:42,290
Now you get a complete thrombus,

141
00:06:42,290 --> 00:06:45,250
and this complete thrombus blocks off the blood supply,

142
00:06:45,250 --> 00:06:48,050
resulting in a lack of flow into the myocardium,

143
00:06:48,050 --> 00:06:50,610
which will then result in death of the myocardium

144
00:06:50,610 --> 00:06:53,090
after 20 minutes of lack of flow.

145
00:06:53,090 --> 00:06:56,530
And so then you'd get necrosis of the cardiac myocytes.

146
00:06:56,530 --> 00:06:59,170
Now other causes include coronary artery vasospasm.

147
00:06:59,170 --> 00:07:02,450
If you had Prinsmetals angina that squeezed down

148
00:07:02,450 --> 00:07:04,930
and shut down the blood vessel for greater than 20 minutes,

149
00:07:04,930 --> 00:07:06,850
you'd get myocardial infarction.

150
00:07:06,850 --> 00:07:07,810
Emboli can do this.

151
00:07:07,810 --> 00:07:10,450
If there was an embolus that went into the coronary artery,

152
00:07:10,450 --> 00:07:12,770
that would result in myocardial infarction.

153
00:07:12,770 --> 00:07:14,850
Another important cause is vasculitis.

154
00:07:14,850 --> 00:07:17,330
The classic example would be Kawasaki's disease.

155
00:07:17,330 --> 00:07:20,770
Remember that that's a vasculitis that affects children

156
00:07:20,770 --> 00:07:22,370
roughly around the age of five

157
00:07:22,370 --> 00:07:25,410
and preferentially involves the coronary artery.

158
00:07:25,410 --> 00:07:28,130
Now the idea here is that if you've got the coronary artery

159
00:07:28,130 --> 00:07:29,570
going into the myocardium

160
00:07:29,570 --> 00:07:32,610
and you have vasculitis or inflammation of this wall,

161
00:07:32,610 --> 00:07:35,170
the inflammation would damage the endothelium,

162
00:07:35,170 --> 00:07:38,370
exposing subendothelial collagen and tissue factor.

163
00:07:38,370 --> 00:07:40,850
This would then allow for the coagulation cascade

164
00:07:40,850 --> 00:07:43,090
to develop a thrombus which could then create

165
00:07:43,090 --> 00:07:44,930
a myocardial infarction.

166
00:07:44,930 --> 00:07:47,010
Here's a gross image of a coronary artery

167
00:07:47,010 --> 00:07:49,410
reminding you of the fact that myocardial infarction

168
00:07:49,410 --> 00:07:51,810
classically occurs when you have rupture

169
00:07:51,810 --> 00:07:54,770
of an atherosclerotic plaque with complete occlusion.

170
00:07:54,770 --> 00:07:58,290
Again, complete occlusion of the coronary artery

171
00:07:58,290 --> 00:08:00,290
by thrombosis.

172
00:08:00,290 --> 00:08:02,450
Clinically, patients who have myocardial infarction

173
00:08:02,450 --> 00:08:04,690
will have severe crushing chest pain

174
00:08:04,690 --> 00:08:06,450
that radiates to the left arm or jaw

175
00:08:06,450 --> 00:08:08,930
and this time it's gonna last greater than 20 minutes.

176
00:08:08,930 --> 00:08:11,010
And again, greater than 20 minutes implies

177
00:08:11,010 --> 00:08:13,650
that you've now begun to cause necrosis

178
00:08:13,650 --> 00:08:15,410
of the cardiac myocytes.

179
00:08:15,410 --> 00:08:18,370
The patients will also have diaphoresis and dyspnea.

180
00:08:18,370 --> 00:08:20,450
Dyspnea is due to the fact that the heart's not pumping

181
00:08:20,450 --> 00:08:22,930
as well and so you're gonna get pulmonary congestion

182
00:08:22,930 --> 00:08:25,490
and edema which is gonna result in dyspnea.

183
00:08:25,490 --> 00:08:27,970
And of course, the symptoms would not be relieved

184
00:08:27,970 --> 00:08:29,650
by nitroglycerin.

185
00:08:29,650 --> 00:08:32,770
Myocardial infarction usually involves the left ventricle,

186
00:08:32,770 --> 00:08:35,970
the right ventricle, and the atria are generally spared.

187
00:08:35,970 --> 00:08:38,290
It's very important to note if we draw a picture

188
00:08:38,290 --> 00:08:39,810
of the heart and cross section,

189
00:08:39,810 --> 00:08:41,490
so this is the right ventricle,

190
00:08:41,490 --> 00:08:43,170
this is the left ventricle,

191
00:08:43,170 --> 00:08:46,130
this would be anterior and this would be posterior.

192
00:08:46,130 --> 00:08:48,050
And so we've sorta just cut across the heart

193
00:08:48,050 --> 00:08:49,970
and we've laid down a piece to look at

194
00:08:49,970 --> 00:08:51,490
in this particular image.

195
00:08:51,490 --> 00:08:53,730
Now, the most common artery involved

196
00:08:53,730 --> 00:08:57,250
in myocardial infarction would be the left anterior descending

197
00:08:57,250 --> 00:08:59,170
which would run somewhere here.

198
00:08:59,170 --> 00:09:01,170
And remember that when you get occlusion

199
00:09:01,170 --> 00:09:02,770
of the left anterior descending,

200
00:09:02,770 --> 00:09:05,090
you're gonna get infarction of the anterior wall

201
00:09:05,090 --> 00:09:08,290
of the ventricle along with the anterior portion

202
00:09:08,290 --> 00:09:10,610
of the intraventricular septum.

203
00:09:10,610 --> 00:09:13,570
The second most commonly involved coronary artery

204
00:09:13,570 --> 00:09:15,970
would be the right coronary artery.

205
00:09:15,970 --> 00:09:18,770
Remember that the right coronary artery would run here

206
00:09:18,770 --> 00:09:21,890
and so when you get occlusion of the right coronary artery,

207
00:09:21,890 --> 00:09:24,930
you're gonna get infarction of the posterior wall

208
00:09:24,930 --> 00:09:26,210
of the left ventricle

209
00:09:26,210 --> 00:09:30,930
and the posterior portion of the intraventricular septum.

210
00:09:30,930 --> 00:09:33,330
And finally the third artery that could be involved

211
00:09:33,330 --> 00:09:34,850
would be the left circumflex

212
00:09:34,850 --> 00:09:37,970
and occlusion of this artery would give you infarction

213
00:09:37,970 --> 00:09:41,570
of the lateral wall of the left ventricle.

214
00:09:41,570 --> 00:09:44,450
And so these are some key arteries that can be involved

215
00:09:44,450 --> 00:09:48,050
with the distribution in which you would see a disease.

216
00:09:48,050 --> 00:09:50,290
When a patient has a myocardial infarction,

217
00:09:50,290 --> 00:09:53,250
the very initial phase of involvement is subendocardial

218
00:09:53,250 --> 00:09:56,530
involving less than 50% of the myocardial thickness.

219
00:09:56,530 --> 00:09:59,410
The idea here is if you've got the endocardium,

220
00:09:59,410 --> 00:10:02,690
the myocardium, and the epicardium,

221
00:10:02,690 --> 00:10:04,690
of course the coronary artery is sitting here

222
00:10:04,690 --> 00:10:09,010
in the epicardium and will be feeding the wall in this manner.

223
00:10:09,010 --> 00:10:11,890
And so the most distal portion from the beginning

224
00:10:11,890 --> 00:10:15,170
of the coronary artery, which is over here in the endocardium,

225
00:10:15,170 --> 00:10:16,770
will be the first to necrose.

226
00:10:16,770 --> 00:10:20,290
And so initially patients will have subendocardial necrosis,

227
00:10:20,290 --> 00:10:23,170
classically involving less than 50% of the wall.

228
00:10:23,170 --> 00:10:25,650
Now, if an EKG were to be done at this point,

229
00:10:25,650 --> 00:10:27,410
it would show ST segment depression.

230
00:10:27,410 --> 00:10:29,890
Again, when you damage the subendocardial region,

231
00:10:29,890 --> 00:10:31,490
you get ST segment depression.

232
00:10:31,490 --> 00:10:34,290
With continued ischemia, this necrosis will progress

233
00:10:34,290 --> 00:10:35,730
and will involve the entire wall,

234
00:10:35,730 --> 00:10:37,810
and that's called a transmural infarction.

235
00:10:37,810 --> 00:10:40,930
Transmural infarctions are the most common type of infarction.

236
00:10:40,930 --> 00:10:41,970
And in this particular case,

237
00:10:41,970 --> 00:10:45,490
you would now see ST segment elevation on the EKG,

238
00:10:45,490 --> 00:10:47,490
and that's because you see ST segment elevation

239
00:10:47,490 --> 00:10:50,130
when you have damage to the entire wall,

240
00:10:50,130 --> 00:10:51,890
as we've already mentioned earlier.

241
00:10:51,890 --> 00:10:54,370
When you want to make a diagnosis of myocardial infarction,

242
00:10:54,370 --> 00:10:57,970
looking for cardiac enzymes in the blood can be very helpful.

243
00:10:57,970 --> 00:11:00,130
Again, the idea here is that you're looking to see

244
00:11:00,130 --> 00:11:03,250
whether there's been irreversible damage to the myocytes,

245
00:11:03,250 --> 00:11:05,650
which is the definition of myocardial infarction.

246
00:11:05,650 --> 00:11:07,810
And when there's irreversible damage to the myocytes,

247
00:11:07,810 --> 00:11:10,130
you know that the hallmark is going to be membrane damage,

248
00:11:10,130 --> 00:11:11,250
and with the membrane damage,

249
00:11:11,250 --> 00:11:13,890
the enzymes that are present within the cardiac myocytes

250
00:11:13,890 --> 00:11:15,490
will leak out into the blood.

251
00:11:15,490 --> 00:11:17,330
And so, if you can find them in the blood,

252
00:11:17,330 --> 00:11:19,810
that tells you that the patient had a myocardial infarction.

253
00:11:19,810 --> 00:11:23,410
The most sensitive and specific marker is troponin I.

254
00:11:23,410 --> 00:11:26,050
This rises two to four hours after infarction,

255
00:11:26,050 --> 00:11:29,810
peaks at 24 hours, and stays high for seven to 10 days.

256
00:11:29,810 --> 00:11:31,810
And so, this really is the best marker.

257
00:11:31,810 --> 00:11:33,810
Another useful marker can be CKMB.

258
00:11:33,810 --> 00:11:38,130
Now, this is useful because it rises quickly,

259
00:11:38,130 --> 00:11:40,610
however, it also goes down relatively quickly.

260
00:11:40,610 --> 00:11:43,090
And so, let's say a patient has an infarction,

261
00:11:43,090 --> 00:11:45,730
and then five days later, develops another infarction,

262
00:11:45,730 --> 00:11:49,090
which can occur, and it's a complication of myocardial infarction.

263
00:11:49,090 --> 00:11:51,650
If you were looking to prove that you had a second infarction,

264
00:11:51,650 --> 00:11:53,570
you wouldn't be able to do so with troponin I,

265
00:11:53,570 --> 00:11:55,650
because troponin I would go up and then stay up

266
00:11:55,650 --> 00:11:57,170
for seven to 10 days.

267
00:11:57,170 --> 00:12:00,690
And so, one potentially useful marker would be CKMB.

268
00:12:00,690 --> 00:12:03,330
And again, the reason being is that it would have gone down

269
00:12:03,330 --> 00:12:06,050
after 72 hours, and if there's a re-infarction,

270
00:12:06,050 --> 00:12:07,410
it will then rise again.

271
00:12:07,410 --> 00:12:11,890
And so, CKMB is particularly useful for detecting re-infarction

272
00:12:11,890 --> 00:12:14,450
a few days after an initial MI.

273
00:12:14,450 --> 00:12:17,490
The treatment for myocardial infarction is aspirin and heparin.

274
00:12:17,490 --> 00:12:20,050
The goal here is to limit additional thrombosis.

275
00:12:20,050 --> 00:12:23,650
Recall that it was the complete occlusion of the coronary artery

276
00:12:23,650 --> 00:12:25,410
by thrombosis that led to the MI,

277
00:12:25,410 --> 00:12:27,730
and so we want to limit the thrombosis.

278
00:12:27,730 --> 00:12:31,170
Supplemental oxygen is given to minimize ischemia.

279
00:12:31,170 --> 00:12:37,090
Nitrates are given with the goal of vasodilating both arteries and veins.

280
00:12:37,090 --> 00:12:40,850
Of course, the major mechanism would be dilatation of the veins,

281
00:12:40,850 --> 00:12:42,930
which would decrease the preload on the heart,

282
00:12:42,930 --> 00:12:45,250
which would then decrease the stress on the heart.

283
00:12:45,250 --> 00:12:47,570
Beta blockers are useful because they slow the heart rate,

284
00:12:47,570 --> 00:12:48,610
and when you slow the heart rate,

285
00:12:48,610 --> 00:12:50,610
you decrease the demand for oxygen,

286
00:12:50,610 --> 00:12:52,610
and you also decrease the risk for arrhythmia.

287
00:12:52,610 --> 00:12:57,890
And arrhythmia is a feared complication very early after MI occurs.

288
00:12:57,890 --> 00:13:00,290
The final treatment listed here would be an ACE inhibitor.

289
00:13:00,290 --> 00:13:04,210
The idea of the ACE inhibitor is that you want to decrease the left ventricular dilatation.

290
00:13:04,770 --> 00:13:09,890
Basically, remember that ACE inhibitors are going to block the production of angiotensin II.

291
00:13:09,890 --> 00:13:12,370
Now, of course, angiotensin II does two things.

292
00:13:12,370 --> 00:13:15,970
Number one, it causes constriction of the peripheral arterioles,

293
00:13:15,970 --> 00:13:18,210
and so therefore, by giving an ACE inhibitor,

294
00:13:18,210 --> 00:13:21,330
you'll block that constriction of the peripheral arterioles,

295
00:13:21,330 --> 00:13:23,810
therefore decreasing the afterload on the heart.

296
00:13:23,810 --> 00:13:28,610
And in addition, remember that angiotensin II goes to the adrenal and causes release of aldosterone,

297
00:13:28,610 --> 00:13:32,450
which eventually comes back to the kidney and grabs sodium, and then water will follow.

298
00:13:32,450 --> 00:13:37,490
So you would increase the blood volume via angiotensin II, and by giving an ACE inhibitor,

299
00:13:37,490 --> 00:13:40,370
you'll decrease that effect so that the blood volume will not increase.

300
00:13:40,370 --> 00:13:45,730
All of this acts to decrease the risk of left ventricular dilatation.

301
00:13:45,730 --> 00:13:49,490
A more definitive treatment would be fibrinolysis or angioplasty.

302
00:13:49,490 --> 00:13:52,290
Remember that the myocardial infarction was due to thrombosis,

303
00:13:52,290 --> 00:13:54,850
and so fibrinolysis would destroy the thrombus.

304
00:13:55,410 --> 00:13:58,210
Now, when you perform fibrinolysis or angioplasty,

305
00:13:58,210 --> 00:14:02,050
that's going to result in a return of blood flow to an area of dead heart,

306
00:14:02,050 --> 00:14:04,610
and so that's going to produce two interesting complications.

307
00:14:04,610 --> 00:14:07,090
The first is called contraction band necrosis,

308
00:14:07,090 --> 00:14:10,930
and the idea here is that if this is the dead myocyte and you return blood,

309
00:14:10,930 --> 00:14:13,330
when you return blood, you're going to return calcium.

310
00:14:13,330 --> 00:14:16,850
So calcium will enter into the cell, and when calcium enters into the cell,

311
00:14:16,850 --> 00:14:21,170
it's going to cause contraction of the muscle fibers within the cell,

312
00:14:21,170 --> 00:14:26,130
and that contraction is then going to result in these dense pink lines within the cells

313
00:14:26,130 --> 00:14:28,210
called contraction bands.

314
00:14:28,210 --> 00:14:30,370
And so here's an image here just to highlight that.

315
00:14:30,370 --> 00:14:31,650
This is an area of dead heart.

316
00:14:31,650 --> 00:14:34,370
You can see that you've got myocytes with no nuclei.

317
00:14:34,370 --> 00:14:36,210
Notice that, for example, this is a myocyte,

318
00:14:36,210 --> 00:14:39,010
and there is no nucleus, indicating that the cell is dead.

319
00:14:39,010 --> 00:14:42,690
However, also notice that you've got these pink bands running up and down the cell,

320
00:14:42,690 --> 00:14:44,610
and this is called contraction bands,

321
00:14:44,610 --> 00:14:47,570
and this occurs when you return calcium back to the dead tissue.

322
00:14:47,570 --> 00:14:51,810
Of course, the calcium then enters the cell and causes contraction of the muscle.

323
00:14:51,810 --> 00:14:56,210
Another interesting complication of returning blood back to the dead tissue

324
00:14:56,210 --> 00:14:58,450
will be that you can get reperfusion injury.

325
00:14:58,450 --> 00:15:01,650
Now, the idea here is that when you return blood, you're returning oxygen,

326
00:15:01,650 --> 00:15:04,930
and the oxygen can then result in the generation of free radicals.

327
00:15:04,930 --> 00:15:08,530
The free radicals can then further injure the myocardium,

328
00:15:08,530 --> 00:15:10,450
and that's called a reperfusion injury.

329
00:15:10,450 --> 00:15:13,090
The way this would present is that you've got a patient who,

330
00:15:13,090 --> 00:15:17,170
for example, has an elevated level of cardiac enzymes.

331
00:15:17,170 --> 00:15:19,970
You then go in and open up the blocked blood vessel,

332
00:15:19,970 --> 00:15:22,210
and then after you open the blocked vessel,

333
00:15:22,210 --> 00:15:24,450
the cardiac enzymes continue to rise.

334
00:15:24,450 --> 00:15:26,690
And so examiners can ask you what's going on,

335
00:15:26,690 --> 00:15:30,210
and the answer is that this is an example of reperfusion injury.

336
00:15:30,210 --> 00:15:32,370
When you gave back blood, you gave back oxygen,

337
00:15:32,370 --> 00:15:35,970
and the oxygen eventually resulted in the formation of free radicals,

338
00:15:35,970 --> 00:15:38,450
which then further damaged the myocardium.

339
00:15:38,450 --> 00:15:40,610
Again, reperfusion injury is very high yield.

340
00:15:41,890 --> 00:15:44,770
Now, the last discussion concerning myocardial infarction

341
00:15:44,770 --> 00:15:47,890
essentially involves both the microscopic and the gross changes

342
00:15:47,890 --> 00:15:50,370
that would occur after the infarction,

343
00:15:50,370 --> 00:15:53,010
along with the complications that you can see clinically

344
00:15:53,010 --> 00:15:55,250
that are basically a result of these changes.

345
00:15:55,250 --> 00:15:57,090
And this is a very high yield table,

346
00:15:57,090 --> 00:15:59,810
so I just want to spend a couple of minutes going over it.

347
00:15:59,810 --> 00:16:01,330
The first thing I'd like to remind you of

348
00:16:01,330 --> 00:16:02,690
is just a general principle.

349
00:16:02,690 --> 00:16:05,090
We're really going to focus here on these microscopic changes,

350
00:16:05,090 --> 00:16:07,810
and everything else will be a derivative of that.

351
00:16:07,810 --> 00:16:09,170
Let's go back and remind ourselves

352
00:16:09,170 --> 00:16:12,930
from the global picture of ischemia and necrosis,

353
00:16:12,930 --> 00:16:14,290
that the first thing that's going to happen

354
00:16:14,290 --> 00:16:17,250
when you cut the blood supply to the cells and the cells die

355
00:16:17,250 --> 00:16:19,410
is you're going to get coagulative necrosis.

356
00:16:19,410 --> 00:16:21,730
Now, coagulative necrosis basically implies

357
00:16:21,730 --> 00:16:24,690
that you're going to remove the nucleus from the cell.

358
00:16:24,690 --> 00:16:26,450
Remember that the hallmark of the dead cell

359
00:16:26,450 --> 00:16:28,210
is removal of the nucleus,

360
00:16:28,210 --> 00:16:29,730
and so you're going to get pycnosis,

361
00:16:29,730 --> 00:16:31,650
cariorexis, and cariolisis.

362
00:16:31,650 --> 00:16:34,770
And that is going to be your sign of coagulative necrosis.

363
00:16:34,770 --> 00:16:37,090
So that's the first thing that's going to happen.

364
00:16:37,090 --> 00:16:38,530
The second thing that's going to happen

365
00:16:38,530 --> 00:16:41,650
is that acute inflammation always follows necrosis.

366
00:16:41,650 --> 00:16:44,290
And so you're going to bring in neutrophils and macrophages.

367
00:16:44,290 --> 00:16:45,810
Now, after you get acute inflammation,

368
00:16:45,810 --> 00:16:47,970
which is going to clean up all of the dead tissue,

369
00:16:47,970 --> 00:16:49,490
the next phase will be healing.

370
00:16:49,490 --> 00:16:51,650
And because the heart is a permanent tissue,

371
00:16:51,650 --> 00:16:53,890
you're not going to be able to regenerate the heart.

372
00:16:53,890 --> 00:16:55,890
Instead, you're going to form scar.

373
00:16:55,890 --> 00:16:58,930
And remember that the foundation of scar is granulation tissue.

374
00:16:58,930 --> 00:17:01,250
And so the next phase will be granulation tissue,

375
00:17:01,250 --> 00:17:04,370
and then finally the granulation tissue will be converted to a scar.

376
00:17:04,930 --> 00:17:06,450
So again, three key phases.

377
00:17:06,450 --> 00:17:09,650
Coagulative necrosis, inflammation, and healing.

378
00:17:10,130 --> 00:17:12,290
Now, the next point is that you have to understand

379
00:17:12,290 --> 00:17:14,290
when each of these phases is going to occur.

380
00:17:14,930 --> 00:17:16,770
Now, the way by which I like to remember this

381
00:17:16,770 --> 00:17:23,250
is to simply remember one day, one week, one month.

382
00:17:25,700 --> 00:17:26,900
And so what's going to happen?

383
00:17:26,900 --> 00:17:30,260
For the first day, you're going to get coagulative necrosis.

384
00:17:30,260 --> 00:17:33,300
After that first day, then you're going to get inflammation

385
00:17:34,180 --> 00:17:35,620
for up to one week.

386
00:17:35,620 --> 00:17:38,020
And the inflammation will begin with neutrophils

387
00:17:38,020 --> 00:17:40,020
and then progress to macrophages.

388
00:17:40,020 --> 00:17:42,740
And after one week, you're going to get granulation tissue.

389
00:17:42,740 --> 00:17:44,740
And then it's going to take one month

390
00:17:44,740 --> 00:17:48,580
for that granulation tissue to be converted to scar.

391
00:17:48,580 --> 00:17:50,820
All right, so one day, one week, one month.

392
00:17:50,820 --> 00:17:54,020
And those are the key sort of dividers of the major phases

393
00:17:54,020 --> 00:17:56,100
that are going to occur during the process.

394
00:17:56,100 --> 00:17:58,500
Now, with that in mind, let's look at the chart.

395
00:17:58,500 --> 00:17:59,460
So what do we say?

396
00:17:59,460 --> 00:18:01,700
We say that for the first 24 hours,

397
00:18:01,700 --> 00:18:04,580
the key thing that's going to happen is coagulative necrosis.

398
00:18:04,580 --> 00:18:06,020
Now, during the first four hours,

399
00:18:06,020 --> 00:18:07,620
you really don't get any changes at all.

400
00:18:07,620 --> 00:18:09,060
There are no microscopic changes,

401
00:18:09,060 --> 00:18:10,980
hence there will be no gross changes.

402
00:18:10,980 --> 00:18:13,140
The key complication during that first four hours

403
00:18:13,140 --> 00:18:14,580
is going to be cardiogenic shock.

404
00:18:14,580 --> 00:18:16,020
If you have a massive infarction,

405
00:18:16,020 --> 00:18:17,620
the heart will completely fail

406
00:18:17,620 --> 00:18:20,020
and you'll get loss of blood supply to the vital organs,

407
00:18:20,020 --> 00:18:21,780
which is basically called shock.

408
00:18:21,780 --> 00:18:24,420
Again, inability to maintain systemic blood pressure

409
00:18:24,420 --> 00:18:26,580
with lack of blood flow to vital organs.

410
00:18:26,580 --> 00:18:30,100
So that's the one complication in that first four hours.

411
00:18:30,100 --> 00:18:32,900
Another complication that can arise in that first four hours

412
00:18:32,900 --> 00:18:34,740
is you can get congestive heart failure,

413
00:18:34,740 --> 00:18:36,900
which is when blood begins to back up

414
00:18:36,900 --> 00:18:39,140
and is not being pumped forward well.

415
00:18:39,140 --> 00:18:42,580
And so the hallmark of that is a decreased ejection fraction.

416
00:18:42,580 --> 00:18:44,580
And the final complication would be arrhythmia.

417
00:18:44,580 --> 00:18:48,420
Arrhythmia is an early complication that occurs in that first day.

418
00:18:48,420 --> 00:18:49,700
Now, after the first four hours,

419
00:18:49,700 --> 00:18:52,100
you're going to begin the process of coagulative necrosis,

420
00:18:52,100 --> 00:18:53,700
and that's going to take one day.

421
00:18:53,700 --> 00:18:55,700
Again, the hallmark of coagulative necrosis

422
00:18:55,700 --> 00:18:58,660
will be pycnosis, cariorexis, cariolysis,

423
00:18:58,660 --> 00:19:00,820
and what you're going to see on gross exam

424
00:19:00,820 --> 00:19:03,380
will be dark discoloration of the heart.

425
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:06,020
Now, the key complication in that first 24 hours

426
00:19:06,020 --> 00:19:08,020
when you've got the initial injury

427
00:19:08,020 --> 00:19:11,620
along with the coagulative necrosis will be arrhythmia.

428
00:19:11,620 --> 00:19:13,940
And basically, the idea here is that if you've damaged

429
00:19:13,940 --> 00:19:16,020
the conducting systems of the heart,

430
00:19:16,020 --> 00:19:18,580
it's going to become evident in that first 24 hours

431
00:19:18,580 --> 00:19:20,100
when the cells are dying.

432
00:19:20,100 --> 00:19:22,100
And if it doesn't happen in the first 24 hours,

433
00:19:22,100 --> 00:19:24,740
it'd be less likely to occur as you move forward

434
00:19:24,740 --> 00:19:26,420
because if you've damaged it, you've damaged it,

435
00:19:26,420 --> 00:19:29,380
and you would see it within the first 24 hours.

436
00:19:29,380 --> 00:19:31,460
Now, of course, at the milestone of one day,

437
00:19:31,460 --> 00:19:33,620
the next thing that's going to happen over the next week

438
00:19:33,620 --> 00:19:35,940
is going to be acute inflammation.

439
00:19:35,940 --> 00:19:37,780
Remember that from one day to one week,

440
00:19:37,780 --> 00:19:39,540
you're going to have acute inflammation.

441
00:19:39,540 --> 00:19:41,620
The very initial phase of that will be neutrophils,

442
00:19:41,620 --> 00:19:43,700
and they'll come in from day one to day three,

443
00:19:43,700 --> 00:19:44,980
and then the macrophages will come in

444
00:19:44,980 --> 00:19:46,660
from day four to day seven.

445
00:19:46,660 --> 00:19:48,180
Now, just focusing on these two,

446
00:19:48,180 --> 00:19:49,940
the key gross change will be that you have

447
00:19:49,940 --> 00:19:52,580
white blood cells within the myocardium,

448
00:19:52,580 --> 00:19:54,580
and the white blood cells within the myocardium

449
00:19:54,580 --> 00:19:56,340
will result in a yellow pallor.

450
00:19:56,340 --> 00:19:58,900
So, during this entire inflammatory phase,

451
00:19:58,900 --> 00:20:01,060
the gross change will be yellow pallor.

452
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:04,420
Now, the key complications based on this microscopic change

453
00:20:04,420 --> 00:20:06,260
will be dependent on what cell is there.

454
00:20:06,260 --> 00:20:08,420
So, for example, when the neutrophils are there,

455
00:20:08,420 --> 00:20:10,100
the main complication is going to be

456
00:20:10,100 --> 00:20:11,860
fibrinous pericarditis.

457
00:20:11,860 --> 00:20:15,060
Now, the idea here is that if you've got your endocardium,

458
00:20:15,860 --> 00:20:17,460
you've got a myocardium,

459
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:19,220
and then you've got the epicardium,

460
00:20:19,220 --> 00:20:21,380
when you get transmural infarction,

461
00:20:21,380 --> 00:20:23,220
you've killed off the entire wall,

462
00:20:23,220 --> 00:20:24,660
then when the neutrophils come in,

463
00:20:24,660 --> 00:20:25,780
the neutrophils will come in

464
00:20:25,780 --> 00:20:27,780
and inflame this entire wall.

465
00:20:27,780 --> 00:20:30,660
Now, in addition, some of that inflammatory exudate

466
00:20:30,660 --> 00:20:32,660
will actually go out into the pericardium,

467
00:20:32,660 --> 00:20:34,660
which surrounds the epicardium,

468
00:20:34,660 --> 00:20:36,580
and so you'll also get pericarditis

469
00:20:36,580 --> 00:20:39,860
during that day one to day three period.

470
00:20:39,860 --> 00:20:41,860
And the key issue here is that that pericarditis

471
00:20:41,860 --> 00:20:44,180
will present with chest pain and a friction rub.

472
00:20:44,180 --> 00:20:45,700
Now, important to note that you'd only get

473
00:20:45,700 --> 00:20:48,260
fibrinous pericarditis with a transmural infarction

474
00:20:48,260 --> 00:20:50,020
when the entire wall was involved.

475
00:20:50,020 --> 00:20:52,580
If the infarction was subendocardial,

476
00:20:52,580 --> 00:20:54,820
where it didn't go up into the epicardium,

477
00:20:54,820 --> 00:20:56,820
of course, you wouldn't have inflammatory debris

478
00:20:56,820 --> 00:20:58,340
going into the pericardium,

479
00:20:58,340 --> 00:20:59,700
and so, therefore, you wouldn't get

480
00:20:59,700 --> 00:21:01,540
fibrinous pericarditis.

481
00:21:01,540 --> 00:21:04,340
Now, after that is going to be the macrophage phase.

482
00:21:04,340 --> 00:21:06,340
And the key reason the macrophages come in

483
00:21:06,340 --> 00:21:09,780
is to eat up all of the dead and necrotic debris.

484
00:21:09,780 --> 00:21:11,380
Now, when the macrophages eat up

485
00:21:11,380 --> 00:21:13,140
all of the dead and necrotic debris,

486
00:21:13,140 --> 00:21:16,100
that is the time that the wall is going to be weakest.

487
00:21:16,100 --> 00:21:17,620
And so, what's the key complication

488
00:21:17,620 --> 00:21:19,220
during the macrophage phase?

489
00:21:19,220 --> 00:21:20,020
Rupture.

490
00:21:20,020 --> 00:21:22,020
That's the single word you want to remember.

491
00:21:22,020 --> 00:21:23,060
And so, for example,

492
00:21:23,060 --> 00:21:25,460
if you had infarction of the ventricular free wall,

493
00:21:25,460 --> 00:21:28,180
that could rupture, resulting in cardiac tamponade.

494
00:21:28,180 --> 00:21:30,500
Again, the idea here is that if this is the heart,

495
00:21:30,500 --> 00:21:32,420
right, and you've got the left ventricle here.

496
00:21:32,420 --> 00:21:33,940
Let's put this to the left atrium,

497
00:21:33,940 --> 00:21:36,340
the right atrium, and the right ventricle.

498
00:21:36,340 --> 00:21:39,700
If you have infarction here of this ventricular free wall,

499
00:21:39,700 --> 00:21:41,380
and this ruptures, of course,

500
00:21:41,380 --> 00:21:43,700
the heart is surrounded by this pericardium.

501
00:21:43,700 --> 00:21:45,300
Blood would go into the pericardium

502
00:21:45,300 --> 00:21:47,060
and would begin to compress the heart,

503
00:21:47,060 --> 00:21:50,340
and that would result in something called cardiac tamponade.

504
00:21:50,340 --> 00:21:52,820
And so, that's one of the complications of rupture.

505
00:21:52,820 --> 00:21:54,740
Another complication of rupture would be to rupture

506
00:21:54,740 --> 00:21:57,220
the intraventricular septum leading to a shunt.

507
00:21:57,220 --> 00:21:59,940
So, again, if this is your four chambers of the heart,

508
00:21:59,940 --> 00:22:02,340
and this is the right ventricle and left ventricle,

509
00:22:02,340 --> 00:22:04,100
and you've got an infarction here,

510
00:22:04,100 --> 00:22:06,340
if this ruptures, you're now going to create an opening

511
00:22:06,340 --> 00:22:08,100
between the right and left ventricle,

512
00:22:08,100 --> 00:22:10,180
and that could result in shunting of blood

513
00:22:10,180 --> 00:22:12,820
from the left ventricle to the right ventricle.

514
00:22:12,820 --> 00:22:15,300
In addition, you can get rupture of a papillary muscle,

515
00:22:15,300 --> 00:22:17,620
and this would lead to mitral insufficiency.

516
00:22:17,620 --> 00:22:18,980
Here, just illustrating this,

517
00:22:18,980 --> 00:22:21,460
here again is your left ventricle.

518
00:22:21,460 --> 00:22:23,860
Remember that the mitral valve has these strings

519
00:22:23,860 --> 00:22:26,020
that come from it called the cordae,

520
00:22:26,020 --> 00:22:28,180
and the cordae attached to the papillary muscle,

521
00:22:28,180 --> 00:22:32,260
and the papillary muscle helps to close this mitral valve.

522
00:22:32,260 --> 00:22:34,500
If you rupture this papillary muscle,

523
00:22:34,500 --> 00:22:36,660
you're not going to be able to pull close this valve,

524
00:22:36,660 --> 00:22:38,660
and so you're going to get insufficiency

525
00:22:38,660 --> 00:22:41,220
with blood going backwards during systole,

526
00:22:41,220 --> 00:22:44,580
and so that would be an example of mitral insufficiency

527
00:22:44,580 --> 00:22:46,500
with rupture of a papillary muscle.

528
00:22:46,500 --> 00:22:48,980
Very high yield to remember that the papillary muscle

529
00:22:48,980 --> 00:22:51,460
is fed by the right coronary artery,

530
00:22:51,460 --> 00:22:53,300
and so therefore it's with occlusion

531
00:22:53,300 --> 00:22:54,980
of the right coronary artery

532
00:22:54,980 --> 00:22:56,420
that you're going to have the risk

533
00:22:56,420 --> 00:22:59,140
of rupture of the papillary muscle.

534
00:22:59,140 --> 00:23:01,460
Now of course, the next phase will be granulation tissue.

535
00:23:01,460 --> 00:23:02,740
After the macrophages come in,

536
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:05,220
you've got to then create the base for the scar,

537
00:23:05,220 --> 00:23:06,900
and the scaffold or the base for the scar

538
00:23:06,900 --> 00:23:08,580
is called granulation tissue.

539
00:23:08,580 --> 00:23:10,740
So granulation tissue, its hallmark will be

540
00:23:10,740 --> 00:23:14,340
plump fibroblasts, collagen, and blood vessels.

541
00:23:14,340 --> 00:23:16,020
Now because there are so many blood vessels,

542
00:23:16,020 --> 00:23:18,020
what you're going to see on gross examination

543
00:23:18,020 --> 00:23:23,300
is a red border coming in from the outside of the dead tissue.

544
00:23:23,300 --> 00:23:24,900
Now the reason it comes in from the outside

545
00:23:24,900 --> 00:23:26,340
of the dead tissue is the following.

546
00:23:26,340 --> 00:23:28,260
If this is the area of necrosis,

547
00:23:28,260 --> 00:23:29,780
then the blood vessels are going to come

548
00:23:29,780 --> 00:23:31,540
from the normal tissue out here

549
00:23:31,540 --> 00:23:34,660
and begin to grow into the area of necrosis

550
00:23:34,660 --> 00:23:36,420
to form the granulation tissue.

551
00:23:36,420 --> 00:23:37,860
And so initially what you're going to see

552
00:23:37,860 --> 00:23:41,460
is a red border to the area of the dead tissue,

553
00:23:41,460 --> 00:23:44,500
and that is going to be the hallmark of granulation tissue.

554
00:23:44,500 --> 00:23:45,620
And so that's why we say here

555
00:23:45,620 --> 00:23:47,540
that when granulation tissue comes in,

556
00:23:47,540 --> 00:23:51,700
you'll see a red border emerging from the edge of the infarct.

557
00:23:51,700 --> 00:23:54,100
Now the final stage is that the granulation tissue

558
00:23:54,100 --> 00:23:56,500
will be converted to fibrosis or a scar.

559
00:23:56,500 --> 00:23:58,100
And so what you're going to see on gross exam

560
00:23:58,100 --> 00:23:59,380
will be a white scar.

561
00:23:59,380 --> 00:24:03,540
Now recall that the scar is not as strong as the myocardium.

562
00:24:03,540 --> 00:24:06,820
So you took away myocardium and you put in place a scar.

563
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:09,460
So because now you've weakened the wall of the ventricle,

564
00:24:09,460 --> 00:24:11,540
that's going to increase the risk for aneurysm

565
00:24:11,540 --> 00:24:14,180
or balloon-like dilatation of the ventricular wall.

566
00:24:14,180 --> 00:24:15,780
Once you've got a scar,

567
00:24:15,780 --> 00:24:17,620
you're not going to have good movement of the wall.

568
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:18,340
And in addition,

569
00:24:18,340 --> 00:24:19,380
if you have an aneurysm,

570
00:24:19,380 --> 00:24:20,740
that's only going to further complicate

571
00:24:20,740 --> 00:24:23,140
the fact that you're going to have stasis along that wall.

572
00:24:23,140 --> 00:24:26,260
So that's going to increase the risk of a mural thrombus

573
00:24:26,260 --> 00:24:30,340
or a thrombus forming along the wall of the scar.

574
00:24:30,340 --> 00:24:34,660
And finally, one complication that can arise is called Dressler's syndrome.

575
00:24:34,660 --> 00:24:36,500
Now this is a very rare complication,

576
00:24:36,500 --> 00:24:37,940
but it does show up on board exams,

577
00:24:37,940 --> 00:24:39,620
so I want to briefly discuss it.

578
00:24:39,620 --> 00:24:42,100
The idea here is that if you had a transmural infarction

579
00:24:42,100 --> 00:24:44,500
and you had inflammation within the pericardium,

580
00:24:44,500 --> 00:24:46,900
that inflammation in the pericardium could expose

581
00:24:46,900 --> 00:24:49,380
the pericardial antigens to the immune system.

582
00:24:50,100 --> 00:24:53,700
This could then theoretically result in the formation of antibodies

583
00:24:53,700 --> 00:24:55,220
against the pericardium.

584
00:24:55,220 --> 00:24:57,780
Now if you formed antibodies against your own pericardium,

585
00:24:57,780 --> 00:25:00,900
you'd get an autoimmune pericarditis, and that would happen,

586
00:25:00,900 --> 00:25:03,940
for example, six weeks or eight weeks after an infarction

587
00:25:03,940 --> 00:25:07,780
when the immune system had enough time to actually generate those antibodies,

588
00:25:07,780 --> 00:25:09,780
and that is called Dressler's syndrome.

589
00:25:09,780 --> 00:25:14,580
It is a pericarditis that arises six to eight weeks after infarction

590
00:25:14,580 --> 00:25:19,380
due to a autoimmune phenomena where you begin to produce antibodies

591
00:25:19,380 --> 00:25:21,140
against your own pericardium.

592
00:25:21,140 --> 00:25:22,660
Again, that is Dressler's syndrome,

593
00:25:22,660 --> 00:25:25,060
and that is very high yield for the board exams.

594
00:25:25,060 --> 00:25:27,060
Alright, now let's use a series of images

595
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:29,140
that is present within your text in order to review

596
00:25:29,140 --> 00:25:31,300
some of these high yield principles.

597
00:25:31,300 --> 00:25:35,220
Here's an example of a heart that has undergone a myocardial infarction.

598
00:25:35,220 --> 00:25:40,260
You can clearly see that there is dark discoloration of the myocardium,

599
00:25:40,260 --> 00:25:45,220
and so we would expect to see dark discoloration from four hours to 24 hours.

600
00:25:45,220 --> 00:25:47,220
And of course, what's happening microscopically

601
00:25:47,220 --> 00:25:51,700
is that we're getting coagulative necrosis from four hours to 24 hours.

602
00:25:51,700 --> 00:25:54,020
Now also you can note that in this particular case,

603
00:25:54,020 --> 00:25:55,940
here is the thickness of the myocardium,

604
00:25:55,940 --> 00:25:59,300
and you can see that the myocardium is only partially involved.

605
00:25:59,300 --> 00:26:00,500
And so in this particular case,

606
00:26:00,500 --> 00:26:02,740
we only have a subendocardial infarction,

607
00:26:02,740 --> 00:26:04,900
at least focally within this image.

608
00:26:04,900 --> 00:26:07,380
Here's a microscopic image showing an infarction.

609
00:26:07,380 --> 00:26:08,740
These are the myocytes,

610
00:26:08,740 --> 00:26:11,700
and you can see that there are no nuclei within the myocytes.

611
00:26:11,700 --> 00:26:15,220
So the fact that the nuclei have been removed indicates that we are,

612
00:26:15,220 --> 00:26:17,940
indicates that coagulative necrosis has occurred.

613
00:26:17,940 --> 00:26:21,460
And of course, coagulative necrosis would occur from four to 24 hours.

614
00:26:21,460 --> 00:26:23,620
Now notice that we don't have any neutrophils here,

615
00:26:23,620 --> 00:26:27,060
and so therefore, we're in that four to 24 hour window.

616
00:26:27,060 --> 00:26:28,660
Now after coagulative necrosis,

617
00:26:28,660 --> 00:26:31,300
the next thing that will happen is that the neutrophils will come in.

618
00:26:31,300 --> 00:26:34,260
And of course, the neutrophils come in from day one to day three,

619
00:26:34,260 --> 00:26:36,900
and the key complication after the neutrophils come in,

620
00:26:36,900 --> 00:26:39,700
will be that we can develop fibrinous pericarditis.

621
00:26:39,700 --> 00:26:43,300
And that's because the inflammation can spread into the pericardium,

622
00:26:43,300 --> 00:26:46,660
resulting in all of this fibrin or inflammatory debris

623
00:26:46,660 --> 00:26:48,260
on the surface of the heart.

624
00:26:48,260 --> 00:26:52,260
So notice that you can kind of see this shaggy appearance to the surface of the heart.

625
00:26:52,260 --> 00:26:55,380
This represents fibrinous pericarditis.

626
00:26:55,380 --> 00:26:59,460
Of course, very high yield to know that fibrinous pericarditis would only occur

627
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:04,260
with a transmural infarction, and you would not see it with a subendocardial infarction.

628
00:27:04,260 --> 00:27:07,620
Now eventually, the next thing that will happen is that the macrophages will come in.

629
00:27:07,620 --> 00:27:10,260
And of course, they come in from day four to day seven.

630
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:13,300
The key complication when the macrophages come in will be rupture.

631
00:27:13,300 --> 00:27:18,020
And so in this particular case, you can see that we've ruptured the free wall of the ventricle,

632
00:27:18,020 --> 00:27:20,420
and so this again would be day four to day seven.

633
00:27:20,420 --> 00:27:23,860
Here's an example where we ruptured the papillary muscle.

634
00:27:23,860 --> 00:27:28,420
Here are the corda tendinae that connect the papillary muscle to the mitral valve.

635
00:27:28,420 --> 00:27:31,060
We've ruptured here the papillary muscle.

636
00:27:31,060 --> 00:27:34,420
Remember that the papillary muscle is fed by the right coronary artery.

637
00:27:34,420 --> 00:27:38,980
And again, because this rupture has occurred, we know that we are day four to day seven.

638
00:27:38,980 --> 00:27:43,940
Of course, after the macrophages come in from one week to one month, we'll have granulation tissue.

639
00:27:43,940 --> 00:27:46,420
And after one month, we'll eventually develop a scar.

640
00:27:46,420 --> 00:27:51,140
And so here you can see a white scar present within the myocardium.

641
00:27:51,140 --> 00:27:55,380
Microscopic examination of that scar would simply reveal dense collagen.

642
00:27:55,380 --> 00:28:00,900
And remember that the key collagen present within the scar is going to be type one collagen.

643
00:28:00,900 --> 00:28:05,620
Now, of course, because you've got a scar instead of myocardium, the wall is potentially weak.

644
00:28:05,620 --> 00:28:11,540
And so over time, that wall can develop a balloon-like dilatation, resulting in a ventricular aneurysm.

645
00:28:11,540 --> 00:28:14,500
And here you can see that we've created a balloon-like dilatation.

646
00:28:15,060 --> 00:28:19,700
You can see that the ventricle is dilated, and again, that is a ventricular aneurysm.

647
00:28:20,260 --> 00:28:25,460
One complication of both scar and aneurysm would be that we could form a neural thrombus

648
00:28:25,460 --> 00:28:27,940
along the wall of the scar or the aneurysm.

649
00:28:28,900 --> 00:28:32,580
The third syndrome of ischemic heart disease is sudden cardiac death.

650
00:28:32,580 --> 00:28:35,940
This is when you get unexpected death due to cardiac disease.

651
00:28:35,940 --> 00:28:40,180
Generally, it would occur without symptoms or with less than one hour of symptoms.

652
00:28:40,740 --> 00:28:43,540
And it's usually due to a fatal ventricular arrhythmia.

653
00:28:43,540 --> 00:28:48,660
And so you get sudden death either without symptoms or very shortly after the onset of symptoms.

654
00:28:49,220 --> 00:28:51,300
The most common etiology is acute ischemia.

655
00:28:51,300 --> 00:28:55,380
Ninety percent of these patients would have pre-existing severe atherosclerosis.

656
00:28:55,380 --> 00:28:57,620
Other causes would include mitral valve prolapse.

657
00:28:57,620 --> 00:28:59,700
That can result in sudden cardiac death.

658
00:28:59,700 --> 00:29:02,820
Cardiomyopathy can result in sudden cardiac death.

659
00:29:02,820 --> 00:29:06,660
And so can cocaine abuse with vasospasm related to cocaine.

660
00:29:06,660 --> 00:29:10,980
But again, most commonly, we would expect to have atherosclerosis.

661
00:29:10,980 --> 00:29:14,660
The fourth and final ischemic heart disease is called chronic ischemic heart disease.

662
00:29:14,660 --> 00:29:19,460
The idea here is that you get poor myocardial function due to chronic ischemic damage.

663
00:29:19,460 --> 00:29:21,780
Now, you don't have to necessarily have infarction.

664
00:29:21,780 --> 00:29:23,300
It can be with or without infarction.

665
00:29:23,300 --> 00:29:26,980
But you have chronic ischemia which slowly damages the myocardium.

666
00:29:26,980 --> 00:29:29,380
This can then progress to congestive heart failure.

667
00:29:29,380 --> 00:29:32,500
And we're going to talk about congestive heart failure in the next section.

668
00:29:32,500 --> 00:29:35,700
And so this concludes our discussion of ischemic heart disease.

669
00:29:35,700 --> 00:29:37,780
And so this concludes our discussion of ischemic heart disease.

670
00:29:37,780 --> 00:29:41,780
So this concludes our discussion of ischemic heart disease.

